 **Parameters:**
-- **$\epsilon$:** Small scalar for the prior term $\epsilon I$ added to keep $H$ invertible [S1].
-- **$\Lambda_r$:** Confidence weight for ray $r$ [S1]. In FisherRF, all rays share the same isotropic value, reducing the formulation to an unweighted squared-error loss [S1].
-- **Performance Metric:** The method evaluates views at 70 fps when using 3D Gaussian Splatting [S2].
-- **Hessian Computation Time:** 11.3 ms ± 33.3 µs for the diagonal Hessian computation [S2].
+*   $\epsilon$: Small scalar for the prior term $\epsilon I$ added to keep $H$ invertible. Value not explicitly given in sources, but standard practice implies a small positive number [S1].
+*   $\Lambda_r$: Confidence weight for ray $r$. In FisherRF, all rays share the same isotropic value, reducing the formulation to an unweighted squared-error loss [S1].
 
 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-- **Sparsity Assumption:** The efficiency relies on the high sparsity of the Hessian due to the disentangling of scene parameters [S2].
-- **Approximation Error:** The use of the trace of the product as an upper bound for entropy decrease is an approximation [S2].
-- **Invertibility:** The full Hessian is intractable to compute and store, handled by keeping only the diagonal elements [S1].
+*   **Intractability:** Computing the full Hessian $H$ is intractable for large models. FisherRF handles this by keeping only the diagonal elements [S1].
+*   **Sparsity:** Matrices are highly sparse due to the disentangling of scene parameters with respect to disparate rays. This sparsity allows for cost-effective computation of the matrix trace (if used) or diagonal updates [S2].
+*   **Independence from Ground Truth:** The Hessian of the objective function in volumetric rendering is independent of ground truth data or actual image measurements. This allows information gain to be computed using only camera parameters of candidate views [S2].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** The computation of the trace is as cost-effective as back-propagation due to matrix sparsity [S2].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
-- **Measured Performance:** Evaluates views at 70 fps with 3D Gaussian Splatting [S2]. The Hessian computation takes 11.3 ms ± 33.3 µs, compared to 1.1 s ± 2.5 ms for alternative methods like Goli et al. [S2].
-- **Strengths:** Direct quantification of uncertainty on model parameters; efficient CUDA implementation for diagonal Hessian computation; state-of-the-art results in view selection and active mapping [S2].
-- **Limitations:** Relies on the sparsity of the Hessian; uses a trace approximation for Information Gain [S2].
-- **When to use:** When active view selection or active mapping is required for Radiance Fields, particularly 3D Gaussian Splatting or Plenoxels [S2].
-- **When not to use:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Time Complexity:** The computation of the diagonal Hessian is as cost-effective as back-propagation [S2].
+*   **Performance:** When using 3D Gaussian Splatting, the method evaluates views at 70 fps [S2].
+*   **Comparison to Alternatives:** Compared to Goli et al., which relies on a hypothetical perturbation field and multiple function calls to the PyTorch backward engine, FisherRF's efficient CUDA implementation for diagonal Hessian computation takes $11.3 \text{ ms} \pm 33.3 \text{ \mu s}$ versus $1.1 \text{ s} \pm 2.5 \text{ ms}$ [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Direct quantification of observed information on model parameters; efficient active view selection; supports batch and path selection [S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on the diagonal approximation of the Hessian, which ignores parameter correlations [S1].
 
 ## Background
-FisherRF was introduced by W. Jiang et al. in the paper "FisherRF: Active View Selection and Mapping with Radiance Fields Using Fisher Information" [S2]. It addresses the problem of active "training" view selection for volumetric rendering, a problem that was extensively studied before radiance fields but lacked literature specific to them [S2]. The method builds on the framework of 3D Gaussian Splatting and Plenoxels, leveraging their differentiable nature and parameter structure [S2]. It improves upon previous methods like Goli et al. by computing the Hessian directly rather than relying on hypothetical perturbation fields and multiple backward passes [S2].
+FisherRF was introduced to address the lack of literature on active "training" view selection for volumetric rendering. It leverages Fisher Information to directly quantify observed information on the parameters of Radiance Fields, selecting candidate views by maximizing Expected Information Gain (EIG) [S2]. The method is implemented on top of point-based 3D Gaussian Splatting and Plenoxels radiance field models [S2]. It is cited as the first method to quantify uncertainty directly on model parameters for Radiance Fields using an efficient formulation [S2].
 
